# term(3)

## NAME

term - draw on the terminal with cursor positions and colours from Lua

## SYNOPSIS

    term.write(text)
    term.blit(text, textColours, backgroundColours)
    term.clear()
    term.clearLine()
    term.scroll(n)
    term.getCursorPos()
    term.setCursorPos(x, y)
    term.getCursorBlink()
    term.setCursorBlink(blink)
    term.getSize()
    term.getTextColour()
    term.setTextColour(colour)
    term.getBackgroundColour()
    term.setBackgroundColour(colour)
    term.isColour()
    term.getPaletteColour(colour)
    term.setPaletteColour(colour, r, g, b)
    term.setPaletteColour(colour, rgb)
    term.nativePaletteColour(colour)
    term.redirect(target)
    term.current()
    term.native()

## DESCRIPTION

lua(1) has a `term` library (also `require("term")`). It follows the `term` API of CC: Tweaked. Every function whose name has `Colour` also has the spelling `Color`.

`term` draws on the terminal of the program (the console of the computer) with escape sequences. Positions start at 1, at the top left. `term.getSize` returns the number of columns and rows of the terminal; when the program is not connected to a terminal, it returns 51 and 19.

`term.setCursorPos` moves the cursor; the position may be outside the screen. `term.write` writes the text at the cursor and moves the cursor to its end. Only the part that is on the screen is drawn: the text does not wrap at the right edge. A wide character (2 cells) that would cross an edge is drawn as a blank cell. Control characters, and bytes that are not valid UTF-8, are drawn as U+FFFD.

`term.blit` writes `text` with colours for each character: `textColours` and `backgroundColours` have one hexadecimal digit (`0` to `f`, see colors(3)) for each character of `text`, and all three must have the same length (`Arguments must be the same length`). A character that is not a hexadecimal digit keeps the current colour.

`term.clear` fills the screen, and `term.clearLine` the line of the cursor, with the background colour. `term.scroll(n)` moves the contents up by `n` lines (down if `n` is negative). They do not move the cursor.

Colours are the values of colors(3). `term.setTextColour` and `term.setBackgroundColour` take effect at once, also for `print` and `io.write`. The 16 colours are drawn with the 16 basic colours of the terminal (`red` with red, `lime` with bright green, and so on), so that they keep their names on a screen with 16 colours. `term.setPaletteColour(colour, r, g, b)` (numbers from 0 to 1) or `term.setPaletteColour(colour, 0xRRGGBB)` gives a colour its own RGB value, which a screen with fewer colours shows as the nearest colour that it has. `term.getPaletteColour` returns the RGB value of the palette, and `term.nativePaletteColour` the default (that of CC: Tweaked). `term.isColour` is always true.

`term.setCursorBlink(false)` hides the cursor. When the program ends normally, the cursor is shown again and the colours of the terminal are reset.

`term.redirect(target)` sends the `term` functions to the table `target` (a table with the same functions, such as a window of your own) and returns the previous target. `term.current` returns the current target, and `term.native` the terminal itself.

## EXAMPLES

    term.setBackgroundColour(colours.blue)
    term.clear()
    term.setCursorPos(3, 2)
    term.setTextColour(colours.yellow)
    term.write("Hello")

## DIFFERENCES FROM CC: TWEAKED

- `print`, `io.write` and `io.read` do not go through `term`. They write where the terminal is, and do not move the cursor of `term`: after `print`, `term.getCursorPos` is not updated. `print` wraps at the right edge and scrolls; `term.write` does not
- Until the first `term.setCursorPos`, the position on the terminal is not known: `term.write` writes where the terminal is, and `term.getCursorPos` is a guess that starts at 1, 1
- The text is UTF-8, and a wide character takes 2 cells. The special characters of CC: Tweaked (128 to 159, such as box drawing and teletext characters) are not available
- The colours that are not changed with `term.setPaletteColour` are drawn with the basic colours of the terminal, not with the RGB values of CC: Tweaked (on a screen with 16 colours, those would turn into grey)
- `term.setPaletteColour` changes only what is drawn afterwards. What is already on the screen keeps its colour
- `term.isColour` is always true, also on a screen with fewer colours (the terminal shows the nearest colour)
- The cursor is shown at the start (CC: Tweaked hides it until `term.setCursorBlink(true)`)
- The screen is not redrawn for the program when the world is loaded: draw it again when the `restored` event arrives (os(3))
- When the program is stopped (for example with Ctrl+C), the colours and the hidden cursor are not reset; `lua -e "term.setCursorBlink(true)"` resets them
- There is no `window` API, and `term.redirect` to a monitor is not available
- Keys that are typed while the program waits for `key` events are also shown on the terminal (see keys(3))

